MINIMUM REMOVE TO MAKE VALID PARENTHESES (Stack + Greedy)

This problem checks real-world string validation, stack usage, and greedy removal.

📌 Problem Statement

Given a string s consisting of lowercase letters and parentheses '(' and ')', remove the minimum number of parentheses so that the resulting string is valid.

A valid parentheses string:

Every '(' has a matching ')'

Parentheses are properly ordered

Return any valid result.

🔹 Example 1
Input:  "lee(t(c)o)de)"
Output: "lee(t(c)o)de"

🔹 Example 2
Input:  "a)b(c)d"
Output: "ab(c)d"

🔹 Example 3
Input:  "))(("
Output: ""

🧠 Core Idea (Interview Gold)

We:

Use a stack to store indices of '('

Remove:

unmatched ')' immediately

leftover '(' after traversal

This guarantees minimum removals.

🛠️ Algorithm (Step-by-Step)
Step 1: Traverse the string

If '(' → push index to stack

If ')':

If stack not empty → pop (valid pair)

Else → mark this ')' for removal

Step 2: Remaining '(' in stack are invalid

Mark all stack indices for removal

Step 3: Build final string

Skip all marked indices

⏱ Complexity

Time: O(n)

Space: O(n)

☕ Java Code (Clean & Interview-Ready)
class Solution {
    public String minRemoveToMakeValid(String s) {
        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
        Set<Integer> remove = new HashSet<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);

            if (c == '(') {
                stack.push(i);
            } else if (c == ')') {
                if (!stack.isEmpty()) {
                    stack.pop();
                } else {
                    remove.add(i);
                }
            }
        }

        // Remaining '(' are invalid
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            remove.add(stack.pop());
        }

        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (!remove.contains(i)) {
                result.append(s.charAt(i));
            }
        }

        return result.toString();
    }
}

